<!-- DOCUMENT 1: PASSWORD HASHING ALGORITHM                       -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<h1>PASSWORD HASHING ALGORITHM</h1>
<p style="font-size: 13pt; font-weight: bold; color: #475569; margin-bottom: 6pt;">
  Secure Login System Using SHA-256 with Salt for MERN Quiz App
</p>

<div class="meta-box">
  <b>Project:</b> MERN Stack QuizHub (Student & Teacher Portals)<br>
  <b>Live Application / Repo:</b> https://github.com/Antony/mern-quiz-app<br>
  <b>Target Architecture:</b> Client-Side Web Crypto API with Salted Digest Storage
</div>

<h2>1. Introduction</h2>
<p>
This login system demonstrates password hashing with a unique random salt using the SHA-256 hash function for both Student and Teacher roles in the MERN Quiz Application. The browser's native Web Crypto API performs the hashing operations. Instead of saving the original password, the system stores only the salt and the resulting hash, so the real password is never kept in the storage or database.
</p>

<h2>2. Algorithm Used</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>Algorithm:</b> SHA-256 (Secure Hash Algorithm, 256-bit) applied to <code>salt + password</code>.</li>
  <li><b>Salt length:</b> 16 bytes (128 bits), generated randomly for each account.</li>
  <li><b>Hash output length:</b> 256 bits (32 bytes), stored as a 64-character hexadecimal string.</li>
</ul>
<p>
SHA-256 is a one-way function: the hash can be calculated from the password, but the password cannot be recovered from the hash. Because plain SHA-256 is very fast, production systems prefer slower algorithms such as PBKDF2, bcrypt, or Argon2. SHA-256 with salt is used here to explain the core idea clearly. It is a standard algorithm and not a newly invented one.
</p>

<h2>3. What Is a Salt?</h2>
<p>
A salt is a random value added to the password before hashing. The salt is not secret and is stored next to the hash. Because every user (whether student or teacher) gets a different salt, two users with the same password get different hashes. This prevents attackers from spotting identical passwords in a leaked database and protects against precomputed hash tables (rainbow tables).
</p>

<h2>4. Registration Algorithm</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Read the username, email, role (Student or Teacher), and password entered by the user.</li>
  <li>Check that all required fields are filled and the password has at least 6 characters.</li>
  <li>Check that the user email does not already exist.</li>
  <li>Generate a secure random 16-byte salt using <code>crypto.getRandomValues()</code>.</li>
  <li>Join the salt and the password and convert them to bytes using <code>TextEncoder</code>.</li>
  <li>Calculate the hash using <code>crypto.subtle.digest("SHA-256", data)</code>.</li>
  <li>Convert the salt and the hash to hexadecimal strings.</li>
  <li>Store the username, email, role, salt, and hash in the browser's localStorage. The password itself is not stored.</li>
</ol>

<h2>5. Login Verification Algorithm</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Read the entered email, password, and selected role.</li>
  <li>Retrieve the stored record for the user; if none exists, reject the login.</li>
  <li>Take the saved salt from the record.</li>
  <li>Join the saved salt with the entered password and hash it with SHA-256.</li>
  <li>Compare the new hash with the stored hash.</li>
  <li>If both match, grant role-based access (Student Dashboard or Teacher Evaluation Dashboard); otherwise show "Wrong password".</li>
</ol>

<h2>6. Simplified Pseudocode</h2>
<div class="code-box">
REGISTRATION(username, email, password, role):
    salt = SECURE_RANDOM_BYTES(16)
    hash = SHA256(salt + password)
    SAVE(username, email, role, salt, hash)
    // Never save the original password

LOGIN(email, entered_password, role):
    record = LOAD(email)
    IF record does not exist:
        RETURN "User not found"
    candidate_hash = SHA256(record.salt + entered_password)
    IF candidate_hash == record.hash:
        SET_SESSION(record.role)
        RETURN "Login successful"
    ELSE:
        RETURN "Wrong password"
</div>

<h2>7. Important JavaScript Web Crypto Functions</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>crypto.getRandomValues()</b> — Generates the random salt using the browser's secure random number generator.</li>
  <li><b>crypto.subtle.digest()</b> — Calculates the SHA-256 hash of the salt and password.</li>
  <li><b>TextEncoder()</b> — Converts the text (salt + password) into bytes before hashing.</li>
  <li><b>localStorage</b> — Stores the user account records and salt-hash pairs in the browser. It is not a secure server-side store.</li>
</ul>

<h2>8. Security Notes and Limitations</h2>
<ul>
  <li>This project is for learning and demonstration. localStorage can be read or changed by the user, so it is not suitable for real production credentials.</li>
  <li>A client-side login check can be bypassed. A real application must verify credentials on a trusted backend server (Express/Node.js) over HTTPS.</li>
  <li>Plain SHA-256 is fast, which helps attackers who try many guesses. Real systems should use bcrypt, PBKDF2, or Argon2id with many iterations.</li>
  <li>The salt does not need to be secret, but the hash should not be exposed unnecessarily.</li>
  <li>A production system should also use rate limiting, generic error messages, secure HTTP-only session cookies, and safe password-reset options.</li>
</ul>

<h2>9. Conclusion</h2>
<p>
The login system uses SHA-256 with a unique 16-byte random salt. During registration, the salt and the hash are saved instead of the original password. During login, the entered password is combined with the stored salt, hashed, and compared with the stored hash. This shows the core principle of salted password hashing, while a production system would add a slower algorithm and server-side verification.
</p>

<div class="author-tag">
  By: Antony [MERN Quiz App Project]
</div>

