<!-- DOCUMENT 2: CI/CD PIPELINE REPORT                            -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<h1>CI/CD PIPELINE REPORT</h1>
<p style="font-size: 13pt; font-weight: bold; color: #475569; margin-bottom: 6pt;">
  GitHub Actions for the MERN Quiz Application
</p>

<div class="meta-box">
  <b>Repository:</b> https://github.com/Antony/mern-quiz-app<br>
  <b>Workflow File:</b> .github/workflows/ci.yml<br>
  <b>Target Platform:</b> GitHub Actions (ubuntu-latest)
</div>

<h2>1. Introduction</h2>
<p>
This report explains the CI/CD pipeline added to my MERN Quiz Application project using GitHub Actions. Every time code is pushed to the repository, GitHub automatically checks the code, runs security hashing checks, verifies the 120-question bank integrity, and builds the application. This catches mistakes early, without checking the files by hand.
</p>

<h2>2. What Is CI/CD?</h2>
<p>
<b>Continuous Integration (CI)</b> means that every code change is automatically built and tested as soon as it is pushed, so errors are found quickly. <b>Continuous Delivery or Deployment (CD)</b> means that code that passes the checks is automatically released to users. In this project, the CI part is the GitHub Actions workflow described below, and the CD part verifies the production build artifacts for deployment.
</p>

<h2>3. Repository Overview</h2>
<p>
The repository contains a full-stack style quiz application built with React, Vite, and Tailwind CSS. It features dual logins (Student and Teacher), 120 curated MERN questions, a teacher performance evaluation dashboard, and Free API integration (Open Trivia DB & Advice Slip). Passwords are saved as salted SHA-256 hashes instead of plain text. The repository contains the application source code in <code>src/</code> and the CI workflow in <code>.github/workflows/ci.yml</code>.
</p>

<h2>4. Tool Used: GitHub Actions</h2>
<p>
GitHub Actions is the automation service built into GitHub. A workflow is a YAML file placed in the <code>.github/workflows</code> folder. It defines when it runs (the trigger), the jobs, and the steps inside each job. Each job runs on a virtual machine called a runner, here <code>ubuntu-latest</code>.
</p>

<h2>5. Workflow File Explanation</h2>
<p>
<b>Name:</b> CI - MERN Quiz Application<br>
<b>Trigger:</b> on push and on pull request to the main branch.<br>
<b>Job:</b> check-and-build, running on ubuntu-latest with the steps given below:
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Step</th>
      <th style="width: 75%;">What it does</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><b>Get the code</b></td>
      <td><code>actions/checkout@v4</code> copies the repository files to the GitHub runner.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>Set up Node.js</b></td>
      <td><code>actions/setup-node@v4</code> installs Node.js 20 on the runner machine.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>Install dependencies</b></td>
      <td>Runs <code>npm ci</code> or <code>npm install</code> to cleanly install all required packages.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>Verify Hashing & Questions</b></td>
      <td>Executes automated verification script testing SHA-256 16-byte salt hashing, password verification, and all 120 MERN questions.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>Build project</b></td>
      <td>Runs <code>npm run build</code> with Vite to compile production-ready assets with zero errors.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>6. Pipeline Flow</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Developer edits the code and pushes it to the main branch.</li>
  <li>GitHub detects the push and starts the workflow automatically.</li>
  <li>A runner is created and the repository code is checked out.</li>
  <li>Node.js setup, dependency install, verification tests, and Vite build run one after another.</li>
  <li>If all steps pass, the run shows a green tick in GitHub Actions.</li>
  <li>If any step fails, the run shows a red cross and the detailed error log is shown immediately.</li>
</ol>

<div class="flow-box">
  Code push &nbsp;&nbsp;&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp; Workflow starts &nbsp;&nbsp;&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp; Automated checks &nbsp;&nbsp;&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp; Pass / Fail &nbsp;&nbsp;&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp; Production Build
</div>

<h2>7. Testing the Pipeline</h2>
<p>
<b>Pass test:</b> The verification script checks that 16-byte salts produce unique 64-character hexadecimal SHA-256 hashes, that all 120 questions (40 Easy, 40 Medium, 40 Hard) are valid, and that Vite builds the bundle in under 3 seconds. The workflow finishes with a green tick across all stages.
</p>

<h2>8. Benefits</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Errors and broken questions are caught immediately after every push.</li>
  <li>Saves time because builds and hashing checks are automatic and repeatable.</li>
  <li>Gives confidence that the main branch always produces working code.</li>
  <li>Free and integrated directly inside GitHub without external servers.</li>
</ul>

<h2>9. Limitations</h2>
<ul>
  <li>The checks verify cryptographic logic, syntax, and build outputs, but do not replace full end-to-end user browser testing.</li>
  <li>Real production enterprise systems would add backend database integration tests and authenticated staging environments.</li>
  <li>GitHub Actions has monthly free tier execution limits for private repositories.</li>
</ul>

<h2>10. Conclusion</h2>
<p>
Adding GitHub Actions made the MERN Quiz Application project more reliable. Every push is automatically checked, and the result is visible as a green tick or a red cross. This demonstrates the core idea of CI/CD: small automatic checks on every change lead to fewer errors, robust security, and faster delivery.
</p>

<div class="author-tag">
  By: Antony [MERN Quiz App Project]
</div>

